<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Saint Pierre and Miquelon</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – present · capital Saint-Pierre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small archipelago off Newfoundland that is the last remnant of French North America, and a fishing outpost since the 18th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French fishermen used the islands from the 16th century. Under the Treaty of Paris of 1763, which ended <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a> on the mainland, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to keep Saint-Pierre and Miquelon as an unfortified shelter for its fishermen on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-banks-of-newfoundland">Grand Banks</a>. Settlers, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a>, arrived soon afterwards.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony depended almost entirely on cod fishing and salting. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> seized and emptied the islands during wars in 1778, 1793 and later, and French rule was only lasting from 1816. In the 1920s Saint-Pierre prospered by smuggling alcohol into the Prohibition-era <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. In 1941 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free French</a> forces took the islands from Vichy control, an early Gaullist success.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The islands became an overseas territory in 1946, an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in 1976 and a territorial collectivity in 1985. The collapse of the cod fishery and a 1992 maritime boundary ruling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada's</a> favour hit the economy hard; the islands still rely on French state support.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saint-pierre-and-miquelon.jpg" alt="Saint Pierre and Miquelon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: André Paturel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saint-Pierre_and_Miquelon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Pierre</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saint-Pierre-et-Miquelon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=saint-pierre-and-miquelon" aria-label="Saint Pierre and Miquelon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3360 534 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saint Pierre and Miquelon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3680 790L5054 728M3680 790L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3680 790h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3680 790h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saint-Pierre. <a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=saint-pierre-and-miquelon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

